          "text": "Consider the sentence: 'The so-called reforms were, in fact, a regression.' A strong analytical step involves isolating the phrase 'so-called.' The evidence is the hyphenated compound 'so-called.' The method involves recognizing this as a marker of skepticism. The analysis explains that by labeling the reforms 'so-called,' the author immediately strips the official terminology of its legitimacy. This choice signals to the audience that the reality does not match the label. The effect is to establish the author's credibility (ethos) as someone who sees through political spin. The step-by-step process is: 1) Quote 'so-called.' 2) Define its connotation (skepticism/demarcation). 3) Connect that connotation to the author's purpose of debunking official narratives. 4) State the audience effect (trust in the author's independent judgment)."
