          "text": "In advanced literary and nonfictional analysis, style encompasses the deliberate choices an author makes regarding diction, syntax, imagery, and tone. Rather than functioning as isolated decorative devices, these elements work in concert to construct arguments, establish credibility, and shape audience reception. Diction refers to specific word choice, which carries denotative meanings as well as connotative resonances that evoke emotional or intellectual responses. Syntax involves sentence structure, length, and arrangement, dictating the pacing and emphasis of a passage. Imagery appeals to the sensory faculties, grounding abstract claims in concrete experience, while tone reveals the author's underlying attitude toward the subject matter. Understanding these choices requires looking past mere identification to evaluate how specific textual evidence directly serves the broader rhetorical purpose and target audience."
