          "text": "A frequent error during revision is substituting ornate vocabulary for clarity, creating inflated or pretentious prose that obscures the logical argument. Another mistake is over-relying on adjectives and adverbs to create intensity, which often signals weak noun and verb selection. Students also tend to alter syntax randomly without considering how clause placement affects emphasis; a subordinate clause placed at the end of a sentence loses the structural stress it might command at the beginning. Always ensure that stylistic enhancements serve the rhetorical purpose and audience expectations rather than existing purely for aesthetic decoration."
