          "type": "text",
          "text": "Sophistication in AP English Language and Composition is not achieved by deploying ornate, elevated vocabulary or dense, decorative prose. Instead, the College Board defines sophistication as a pervasive demonstration of rhetorical understanding through consistently elevated reasoning. Writers attain this level of maturity by engaging deeply with the complexities and tensions inherent in their subject matter, rather than smoothing over nuances for the sake of an easy, simplistic thesis. In analytical and argumentative writing, sophistication manifests when a student avoids black-and-white thinking and instead examines the conditional validity of an argument. This requires acknowledging how context, historical precedent, or underlying assumptions shape the truth value of a claim. By prioritizing logical architecture over stylistic flourishes, a writer demonstrates a mature command of rhetoric that persuades through intellectual rigor rather than emotional or lexical manipulation."
