          "text": "To execute sophisticated reasoning in your own analysis, you must adopt a method that traces how an author constructs a qualified position. First, identify the primary assertion in a text and locate the specific concessions or counterarguments the author introduces. Rather than treating these concessions as weaknesses, analyze them as strategic friction points that test the durability of the main claim. Next, examine the transitional junctures where the author pivots from acknowledging a limitation to reaffirming a nuanced stance. Ask yourself what logical bridge connects the caveat to the ultimate conclusion. By focusing on these structural transitions, you move past mere device identification and begin to evaluate the dynamic relationship between evidence, warrant, and claim. This methodological shift ensures your commentary captures the evolving architecture of the argument rather than summarizing static plot points or surface-level stylistic choices."
