      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nUrban planners often claim that traffic congestion is an inevitable byproduct of population growth. However, data from three comparable mid-sized cities suggests otherwise. In two of these cities, congestion declined by 15% after the implementation of staggered work hours. The remaining city maintained its congestion levels despite similar population increases. This discrepancy highlights a critical failure in metropolitan strategy. It is not the number of people that crushes the grid, but the rigidity of the schedule that does the damage. When planners label congestion as 'unavoidable,' they absolve themselves of the responsibility to innovate. The solution requires a rethinking of daily rhythms, not just the widening of lanes. We must stop blaming the crowd and start examining the clock."
