      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nUrban noise pollution is often dismissed as a mere nuisance, yet its cognitive consequences are profound. Modern cityscapes are saturated with mechanical hums, traffic, and construction, creating a constant auditory backdrop. Research suggests that the human brain is not passive in this environment; it actively tries to filter out unimportant sounds. This filtering process, however, is metabolically expensive. When the brain diverts energy to suppress background chaos, fewer resources remain for higher-order tasks like reading comprehension or mathematical reasoning. Consequently, students in high-noise environments may not be less intelligent, but rather their capacity for deep focus is systematically eroded. The noise does not change their minds; it changes the environment in which their minds operate, creating a persistent barrier to learning that is often invisible to administrators who measure success only by standardized test scores."
