      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nThe relentless march of municipal redevelopment is frequently championed as the ultimate engine of civic progress, sweeping away antiquated structures to make room for sleek efficiency. Yet, beneath the gleaming glass facades and widened boulevards lies a quiet erosion of historical continuity. To simply categorize this transformation as either a triumph of innovation or a tragedy of displacement is to indulge in a lazy binary. In truth, urban renewal operates as a double-edged mechanism: it injects much-needed capital into flagging local economies while simultaneously severing the tangible links that anchor a community to its past. The challenge for modern city planners is not halting progress to preserve decay, but rather engineering a synthesis where economic vitality and historical identity coexist in a delicate, necessary tension. True civic health requires honoring the ghost of what was built before while stubbornly laying the foundations for what must come next."
