      "rubric": "Concession Presence (1 points): Use language that validates the opposition's observation (e.g., \"It is true that...\", \"Critics are right that...\"). Do not negate the fact being conceded.\nRestrictive Rebuttal (4 points): Ensure the rebuttal explains *why* the conceded point is insufficient to prove the claim. Use pivot words like \"however,\" \"yet,\" or \"even so.\" The argument must move beyond the conceded fact to a broader context.\nCoherent Flow (1 points): The sentences should connect logically. The concession should lead naturally into the restriction. Avoid abrupt shifts or fragmented thoughts.",
      "explanation": "Use language that validates the opposition's observation (e.g., \"It is true that...\", \"Critics are right that...\"). Do not negate the fact being conceded.\nEnsure the rebuttal explains *why* the conceded point is insufficient to prove the claim. Use pivot words like \"however,\" \"yet,\" or \"even so.\" The argument must move beyond the conceded fact to a broader context.\nThe sentences should connect logically. The concession should lead naturally into the restriction. Avoid abrupt shifts or fragmented thoughts.",
      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nUrban planners often propose that green roofs should be mandatory in all commercial districts. While this mandate would undoubtedly reduce heat island effects and manage stormwater runoff, critics argue it imposes an undue financial burden on small business owners. Admittedly, the initial cost of installation is significant, and not all structures can support the added weight. However, if those costs were offset by tiered tax incentives based on building size, the financial strain would be mitigated. Consequently, the proposal remains viable only if paired with equitable subsidy mechanisms. Without such support, a blanket mandate would simply penalize lower-margin businesses, thereby shifting the environmental burden onto those least able to bear it. A nuanced approach requires balancing ecological necessity with economic survival."
