      "rubric": "Claim/Thesis (1 points): Must go beyond mere summary to evaluate specific rhetorical strategies like qualification or contrast.\nEvidence and Analysis (4 points): Award points for examining how references to European models and transit dependencies expose source limitations.\nSophistication/Style (1 points): Check for precise vocabulary, logical transitions, and freedom from mechanical errors.",
      "explanation": "Must go beyond mere summary to evaluate specific rhetorical strategies like qualification or contrast.\nAward points for examining how references to European models and transit dependencies expose source limitations.\nCheck for precise vocabulary, logical transitions, and freedom from mechanical errors.",
      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nProponents of municipal downtown pedestrianization routinely celebrate car-free zones as miraculous cures for lagging urban retail sectors. Citing gleaming transformations in European city centers, advocates argue that removing vehicular traffic instantly unlocks vibrant pedestrian foot traffic, elevating shopkeeper revenues. However, these localized success stories frequently ignore the foundational transit ecologies that made those European transformations viable. A pedestrian corridor cannot thrive in isolation; it requires a dense, highly efficient regional rail network to funnel consumers directly into the commercial zone. When municipal planners attempt to replicate these car-free experiments in sprawling, automobile-dependent suburban municipalities devoid of robust mass transit, the results are predictably disastrous. Without systemic infrastructural support, restricting vehicular access simply starves local businesses of patrons, transforming bustling commercial strips into desolate corridors. True urban revitalization demands careful contextual analysis rather than uncritical emulation of foreign models."
