      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nUrban revitalization initiatives frequently stall due to persistent disagreements over resource allocation and spatial priorities. Proponents of downtown commercial development argue that municipal investments must focus on expanding transit networks and modernizing commercial hubs to attract corporate tax revenue and stimulate regional economic growth. Conversely, community advocates contend that such capital-intensive projects marginalize long-term residents by accelerating property tax hikes and commercial displacement. Both factions claim to prioritize the city's long-term sustainability, yet their underlying definitions of progress remain fundamentally incompatible. True urban progress cannot occur until planners reconcile the demand for competitive global positioning with the urgent necessity for equitable neighborhood preservation, ensuring that economic modernization does not inadvertently erode the social fabric it aims to support."
