      "passage": "Original instructional passage; described studies and figures are hypothetical.\n\nModern technological integration in civic spaces is frequently championed as an unmitigated triumph of democratic access. Enthusiasts argue that digital platforms democratize information, ensuring every citizen possesses an equal voice in public discourse. Yet this utopian vision overlooks the profound structural inequalities that govern digital connectivity. While high-speed broadband and algorithmic transparency can indeed foster unprecedented civic participation in affluent urban centers, rural and economically marginalized communities frequently experience digital exclusion rather than empowerment. Therefore, asserting that technology inherently revitalizes democracy is dangerously simplistic. We must instead conclude that digital tools act as powerful amplifiers of existing societal structures; they deepen democratic engagement only where foundational infrastructural equity has already been deliberately established by policy."
